    "threadId": "thr_upzuujiuue",
    "seq": 24,
    "createdAt": 1787030789364,
    "type": "item/completed",
    "data": {
      "providerThreadId": "2d21170a-36c6-4e91-a090-abcf47304bfd",
      "item": {
        "type": "agentMessage",
        "id": "btaf46b272-1-assistant-1",
        "text": "- **Ops burden**: SQLite is a file — zero install, zero daemon, trivially backed up by copying. Postgres means shipping/managing a server process alongside your app, which is a real packaging and upgrade headache on desktop.\n- **Concurrency**: Postgres wins on many concurrent writers, but a single-user desktop app has ~one writer. SQLite in WAL mode handles that plus background readers fine.\n- **Features/types**: Postgres gives you rich types (jsonb, arrays, proper timestamps), real constraints, window/CTE depth, and full-text/extensions. SQLite has dynamic typing, weaker constraint enforcement, and fewer built-ins.\n- **Migrations & schema**: Postgres has full `ALTER TABLE`; SQLite's is limited, so column changes often mean table-rebuild dances. Tooling helps, but it's friction over years of schema churn.\n- **Performance profile**: SQLite is in-process — no network/IPC hop, so simple queries are typically faster. Postgres pulls ahead on complex query plans and large datasets, and has a far better optimizer.\n- **Future path**: If a sync/multi-device/server story is likely, Postgres now avoids a painful port later; if it stays local-first, SQLite is the simpler forever-answer (and Turso/libSQL exist if you want sync on top of SQLite).\n\nQuestion for you: is this app going to stay strictly local-only, or do you expect multi-device sync or a hosted backend within the next year or so?"
      }
    }
  },
